  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH TUESDAY: \nSunshine today with light southerly winds have pushed \ntemperatures into the mid 40s to near 50 already this afternoon.\nWe have high pressure to our east, and considering how dry we \nare with nearly full sun, don't be surprised to see high \ntemperatures overperform relative to guidance today, rising \nabove 50 for a large portion of the area. Dewpoints have been \npushed down for this afternoon, and the generally dry conditions\nwill allow temperatures tonight and Monday night to drop off \nmore than most guidance depicts as well.\n\nSunny to mostly sunny skies will continue for Monday and \nTuesday. Southerly winds will strengthen during the day, with \nmixing bringing down wind gusts in the 20 to 25 mph range both \nMonday and Tuesday. These conditions should help boost high \ntemperatures toward the upper end of guidance for both days, \nsimilar to what we are seeing this afternoon.\n\nClouds will begin increasing on Tuesday as a low pressure \nsystem slowly crosses the Rockies and ejects out onto the Plains\nby Tuesday night. However, no precipitation is expected across \nour area through Tuesday night as the dry airmass holds strong.\n\nWEDNESDAY THROUGH NEXT SUNDAY:\nWe have been discussing a significant pattern change for the \nlater half of this week, which still appears on track. The upper\nlevels become more amplified over the CONUS around mid week as \na long wave trough over the Rockies slides east into the plains.\nThis system pushes a surface low from WY northeast to northwest\nOntario, just skirting the forecast area to the north. This \nsystem will draw moisture up into the area from the south, with \nhigher than normal precipitable water values for this time of \nyear. Rain chances will return to the region Wednesday evening \nand persist into Thursday, though widespread heavy amounts \nappear unlikely with this initial wave.\n\nAfter a brief break Thursday night, a more substantial system \nis expected to affect the area as larger upper level trough \ncrosses the Rockies, developing a surface low over the high \nplains on Friday which pushes northeast into the western Great \nLakes region by Saturday. At this time, precipitation appears to\nprimarily fall as rain, with potential for snow to mix in as \nthe system wraps up heading into the remainder of the weekend.\n\nThe overall confidence in this more active and progressive \npattern is high. However, details regarding precipitation \ntiming, amounts, and type are still quite variable. Some of the \nensemble members are painting a swath of accumulating snow over \nportions of the Northland from the weekend system, though most \nof them keep it farther north, starting in North Dakota and \nextending northeast into Ontario. We will continue to monitor \nthis potential for snowfall to see how that evolves over the \nnext few days. Temperatures turn sharply colder next weekend, \ndropping down to near normal values with highs in the 30s to low\n40s by next Sunday.",
